From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: irisrun <irisrun@localhost>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:07:02 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 13/38] Run the stdout app as a Device Protocol device

With IRISRUN_DEVICE=stdio the app speaks Iris's Device Protocol on stdio, as
irisrun start runs it: size-delimited HostMessage envelopes come in on stdin
and DeviceMessage envelopes go out on stdout, and what the app prints moves to
stderr. It sends its device info and app status, then a JPEG frame whenever a
new display list reaches the render core, drawn at 2x by default and encoded
on another thread, skipping frames the encoder fell behind on.

Touches (in pixels), input method edits and key presses go through
DeviceInput. The main thread runs its run loop, so transactions, timers and
the main queue work, and renders when the view graph asks, at most 60 times
a second, on the clock touch events carry. Input first brings the view
graph's clock up to now: gestures inside views time touches with it, and a
clock left at the last frame, then moved on by a frame in the middle of a
tap, made the tap seem held too long.

OpenSwiftUI depends on Iris's runtimes/ios package for IrisProtocol, through
OPENSWIFTUI_IRIS_RUNTIME_PATH.
